Minimum Viable Experiment

Choose one target such as focus, downshifting, playful energy, or social connection. Build three versions of the same program:

• Sound only
• Breath or movement only
• Cross-modal combination

Record a two-minute baseline, deliver a five-minute pattern, and observe a five-minute recovery. Measure subjective state, breathing rate, heart rate if available, and willingness to repeat. Repeat on several days and change only one primitive at a time.

The goal is not to prove that a universal rhythm exists. The goal is to discover which structural features transfer, which depend on context, and which become part of a reliable personal or group grammar.

Canonical Compression

> Somatic Syntax is the compositional language of timed patterns that bodies interpret as changes in state. Its primitives are pulse, tempo, contour, prediction, variation, breath, effort, synchrony, meaning, and silence. Its programs operate through perception, movement, autonomic regulation, chemistry, memory, and social feedback. Its outputs are probabilistic, contextual, measurable, and transferable across domains. The body is not merely the receiver of the program. The body is part of the compiler.